# Executive Policy Brief

## Title: Building Accountable and Responsible AI Governance in Organisations

**Audience:** Organisational leaders, AI policy teams and governance professionals.

## Executive Summary

Artificial intelligence creates opportunities for innovation and operational efficiency. However, its deployment can introduce risks involving fairness, privacy, reliability, transparency and accountability.

Organisations need governance structures that ensure AI systems are assessed before deployment, monitored during operation and reviewed when risks change.

This brief proposes five practical priorities for responsible AI governance.

## The Governance Challenge

AI governance cannot rely solely on model accuracy or technical performance.

An AI system may perform well on technical benchmarks while still producing unequal outcomes, exposing personal information or generating misleading outputs.

Organisations must therefore consider the broader impacts of AI systems on individuals and society.

## Five Policy Recommendations

### 1. Establish Clear Accountability

Assign ownership for AI systems, risks, incidents and deployment decisions.

### 2. Conduct Pre-Deployment Risk Assessments

Identify affected stakeholders, potential harms and appropriate safeguards before deployment.

### 3. Evaluate Fairness and Reliability

Test systems across relevant groups and use cases. Document limitations and uncertainty.

### 4. Strengthen Human Oversight

Provide meaningful opportunities for human review, intervention and escalation.

### 5. Monitor Systems Continuously

Review AI performance, incidents, complaints and emerging risks throughout the system lifecycle.

## Implementation Priorities

| Timeline | Priority Action |
|---|---|
| Initial phase | Identify AI systems and assign owners |
| Before deployment | Conduct risk and impact assessments |
| Deployment phase | Establish human oversight and monitoring |
| Ongoing | Review incidents, outcomes and safeguards |

## Conclusion

Responsible AI governance requires more than high-level principles. It requires clear institutional responsibilities, evidence-based risk assessment, effective safeguards and continuous oversight.

Organisations should treat governance as an ongoing process rather than a one-time approval exercise.

